course: world
lesson: 45
title: The UK, Ireland, Canada, Australia and New Zealand
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The United Kingdom is made of four nations, England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, on islands off the north-west of Europe; its capital is London on the River Thames, and its mild, rainy weather comes from the ocean all around it.
explain: Ireland is the green island to the west of Great Britain, with Dublin as its capital; its frequent rain keeps the grass green all year. Canada, north of the United States, is the second-largest country in the world, with Ottawa as its capital, huge forests, the Rockies, thousands of lakes and cold Arctic islands.
explain: Australia is a whole continent and a country, with Canberra as its capital; most of its middle, the Outback, is hot dry desert, so most Australians live near the coasts in cities such as Sydney and Melbourne. New Zealand, with Wellington as its capital, is two main mountainous islands to the south-east, with volcanoes, glaciers and fjords.
explain: Australia and New Zealand are in the Southern Hemisphere, so their seasons are upside down: when the north half of the Earth is tilted towards the Sun in July, the south half is tilted away, so July is winter in Melbourne and Christmas comes in the summer, often at the beach.
explain: Because Australia and New Zealand were cut off by the sea for millions of years, they have animals found nowhere else: kangaroos and koalas, which carry their babies in pouches, the egg-laying platypus, and in New Zealand the kiwi, a bird that cannot fly.
fact: The United Kingdom is made of England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.
fact: London, on the River Thames, is the capital of the United Kingdom.
fact: Dublin is the capital of Ireland.
fact: Ottawa is the capital of Canada, and Canada is the second-largest country in the world.
fact: Canberra is the capital of Australia, though Sydney is its largest city.
fact: Wellington is the capital of New Zealand.
fact: Most of the middle of Australia, called the Outback, is hot and dry.
fact: Australia and New Zealand are in the Southern Hemisphere, where the seasons are opposite to those in the north.
fact: In the Southern Hemisphere, December, January and February are summer months.
fact: Kangaroos and koalas are marsupials: their babies grow in a pouch.
fact: The platypus of Australia is a mammal that lays eggs.
fact: The kiwi is a flightless bird of New Zealand.
exercise: Which four nations make up the United Kingdom? | England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland | England, Ireland, Scotland and Canada | England, Wales, Ireland and Iceland | Scotland, Wales, Australia and England
exercise: What is the capital of the United Kingdom? | London | Dublin | Edinburgh | Manchester
exercise: What is the capital of Ireland? | Dublin | Belfast | London | Cork
exercise: What is the capital of Canada? | Ottawa | Toronto | Vancouver | Montreal
exercise: What is the capital of Australia? | Canberra | Sydney | Melbourne | Perth
exercise: What is the capital of New Zealand? | Wellington | Auckland | Sydney | Christchurch
exercise: Why do most Australians live near the coasts? | The middle of the country is hot dry desert | The middle of the country is covered by ice | The law forbids living inland | The coasts are higher and cooler than the mountains
exercise: Why are the seasons in Australia opposite to those in Britain? | The two places are in different halves of the tilted Earth | Australia is much closer to the Sun in July | The Sun goes around Australia the other way | Australia has a different kind of calendar
exercise: In which season is Christmas in Melbourne? | Summer | Winter | Autumn | Spring
exercise: Which animal carries its baby in a pouch? | The kangaroo | The kiwi | The platypus | The moose
exercise: What is unusual about the platypus? | It is a mammal that lays eggs | It is a bird that cannot fly | It is a fish that walks on land | It is a reptile with fur
exercise: Which bird of New Zealand cannot fly? | The kiwi | The eagle | The parrot | The seagull
exercise: Why does Australia have animals found nowhere else? | The sea cut it off for millions of years | Its animals were all brought there by ships | Its hot sun slowly changes any animals that arrive | Animals there all live only in the desert
exercise: Why is Ireland so green all year? | Frequent rain and mild weather keep the grass growing | The soil there is painted green by farmers | The sun never shines there at all, so the grass stays fresh | Its grass is a special kind that never dies
exercise: Which country is the second-largest in the world? | Canada | Australia | The United Kingdom | New Zealand
test: A boy in Melbourne and his cousin in London both look out of the window in January. What do they see? | Summer in Melbourne and winter in London | Winter in both cities | Summer in both cities | Winter in Melbourne and summer in London
test: A girl in Sydney says her city is the capital of Australia. What is true? | Sydney is the largest city but Canberra is the capital | She is right, Sydney is the capital | Melbourne is both the capital and the largest city | Australia has no capital city
test: A girl in Toronto sees a map that shows the country north of the United States, full of lakes and forests. Which country is it? | Canada | Ireland | Australia | New Zealand
test: A zoo keeper shows an animal that has fur, feeds its young on milk and lays eggs. Where does it come from in the wild? | Australia | Canada | Ireland | Scotland
test: If you flew from London to Wellington in June, what would you need to pack for the arrival? | Warm winter clothes | A swimsuit for the summer heat | Only clothes for the spring | Clothes for a dry desert
test: Which is true about the United Kingdom? | It includes Scotland and Wales and its capital is London | It includes Ireland and its capital is Dublin | It lies in the Southern Hemisphere | It is the second-largest country in the world
test: A boy in Auckland goes hiking and sees glaciers, volcanoes and deep fjords on the same holiday. Where is he? | New Zealand | Ireland | Florida | The Australian Outback
test: Why do few people live in the Outback? | It is very hot and has little water | It is covered with thick dark forests | It is far too cold to build any houses | It lies under the sea at every high tide
test: Which animal lives in the wild in New Zealand but not in Canada? | A kiwi | A moose | A beaver | A bear
test: christmas in sydney is it snowy or what | No, it is summer there in December | Yes, it snows there every single Christmas | Yes, December is winter there | It is spring there in December
test: capital of oz, is it sydney | No, it is Canberra, though Sydney is the largest city | Yes, Sydney is the capital | No, it is Melbourne | No, it is Wellington
test: my mate says Ireland's part of the UK. true? | No, Ireland is its own country; only Northern Ireland is in the UK | Yes, all of Ireland is in the UK | Yes, Dublin is in England | No, Northern Ireland is part of Scotland
test: Jake reckons the kiwi's a fruit bat from Australia. right? | No, the kiwi is a flightless bird of New Zealand | Yes, it is a bat that flies at night | Yes, it lives in a pouch | No, it is a marsupial from the forests of Canada
exam: Identify the constituent parts of the United Kingdom. | England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland | England, Scotland, Wales and Ireland | England, Wales and Ireland alone | England and Scotland alone
exam: Identify the capital of Australia. | Canberra | Sydney | Melbourne | Perth
exam: Identify the months constituting summer in the Southern Hemisphere. | December, January and February | June, July and August | March, April and May | September, October and November
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the kangaroo? | A marsupial whose young develop in a pouch | A mammal that lays eggs | A flightless bird of New Zealand that lays eggs | A reptile of the Outback
